WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:13.700
Authors have an obligation to make sure their
paper is based on original, never before published,

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.700
research.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.700
Intentionally submitting or re-submitting
work for duplicate publication is considered

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.030
a breach of publishing ethics.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:25.640
Simultaneous submission occurs when a person
submits a paper to different publications

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:30.119
at the same time, which can result in more
than one journal publishing that particular

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.730
paper.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:36.680
Duplicate or multiple publication occurs when
two or more papers, without full cross-reference,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.590
share essentially the same hypotheses, data,
discussion points, and/or conclusions.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:47.710
This can occur in varying degrees including
literal duplication, partial but substantial

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.360
duplication, or even duplication by paraphrasing.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.350
One of the main reasons duplicate publication
of original research is considered unethical,

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.449
is that it can result in inadvertent double
counting or inappropriate weighting of the

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:06.700
results of a single study, which distorts
the available evidence.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.520
There are certain situations in which the
publishers of two journals might agree in

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.520
advance to use the "duplicate work".

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.520
These include:

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.359
• Combined editorials (e.g. about a plagiarism
case involving the two journals).

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:22.689
• Clinical guidelines, position statements.

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:27.340
• Translations of articles, provided that
prior approval has been granted by the first

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.649
Publisher, and that full and prominent disclosure
of its original source is given at the time

00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.330
of submission.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.890
The main rule of thumb is that articles submitted
for publication must be original and must

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.360
not have been submitted to any other publication.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.579
At the time of submission, authors must disclose
any details of related papers, also when in

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:53.939
a different language, similar papers in press,
and translations.

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:57.880
The “slicing” of research that would form
one meaningful paper into several different

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.399
papers is called "salami publication" or "salami
slicing".

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:07.850
Unlike duplicate publication, which involves
reporting the exact same data in two or more

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:12.420
publications, salami slicing involves breaking
up or segmenting a large study into two or

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.680
more publications.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.250
These segments are referred to as "slices"
of a study.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.220
As a general rule, as long as the "slices"
of a broken up study share the same hypotheses,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.800
population, and methods, this is not acceptable
practice.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:33.580
The same "slice" should never be published
more than once.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.720
The reason, according to the U.S. Office of
Research Integrity, salami slicing can result

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.000
in a distortion of the literature by leading
unsuspecting readers to believe that data

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.050
presented in each salami slice is derived
from a different subject sample.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:52.790
This not only skews the "scientific database"
but it creates repetition that wastes readers'

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.920
time as well as the time of editors and reviewers,
who must handle each paper separately.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.830
Further, it unfairly inflates the author's
citation record.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:09.200
There are instances where data from large
clinical trials and epidemiological studies

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.750
cannot be published simultaneously, or are
such that they address different and distinct

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.510
questions with multiple and unrelated endpoints.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.420
In these cases, it is legitimate to describe
important outcomes of the studies separately.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.830
However, each paper should clearly define
its hypothesis and be presented as one section

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:34.270
of a much larger study.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:39.840
In summary, duplicate publication and salami
slicing are unacceptable practices and should

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.260
be avoided at all costs as it can give an
unfair advantage to some scientists.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.410
By increasing the number of publications they
can list on their resume, scientists and researchers

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.430
can progress faster in their careers or receive
more funding than they actually merit.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:00.340
Additionally, salami slicing also leads to
a waste of time for readers and reviewers

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.650
as they have to go through repetitive content.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.400
It also leads to distortion of scientific
literature.

